      "message": {
       "content": "# Scope and Limitations\n\n## Scope of the Study\n\nThis research is designed to investigate and define the effectiveness of a specialized drilling tool\u2014equipped with integrated alignment guides\u2014in promoting accurate and consistent drilling centers across multiple repeated trials. The study is bounded by the following parameters:\n\n**1. Primary Objective and Focus**\nThe central aim of this investigation is to quantify and characterize the tool's capacity to produce repeatable, centered drill holes under controlled experimental conditions. The research focuses specifically on the geometric accuracy (concentricity, positional deviation, and angular alignment) of the resulting holes relative to the intended center point, measured across a defined number of repeated trials (e.g., 20\u201350 repetitions per test configuration).\n\n**2. Alignment Guide Functionality**\nA dedicated component of the study examines the mechanical and functional performance of the tool's alignment guides. This includes:\n- Evaluating the guides' ability to constrain and direct the drill bit toward the intended center point.\n- Assessing the guides' resistance to wear, deformation, and misalignment over successive uses.\n- Analyzing the interaction between the guide geometry (e.g., V-grooves, pin locators, or magnetic clamps) and the drill bit's entry angle.\n- Measuring the degree to which the guides reduce operator-dependent variability in hole placement.\n\n**3. Workpiece Dimensions**\nThe study assesses the tool's performance across a defined range of workpiece geometries, including:\n- Variation in workpiece thickness (e.g., 3 mm to 25 mm).\n- Variation in workpiece diameter or cross-sectional area (e.g., cylindrical, square, and rectangular profiles).\n- Edge proximity effects",
